Building high-end web experiences from scratch is often a waste of time.

I used to spend entire weekends manually tweaking CSS transitions or fighting with WebGL shaders just to get a simple hover state or a scroll interaction to feel right. It was a massive sink of time that rarely added actual value to the user experience.

I have been testing three libraries recently that let you pull in premium, interactive components directly into your codebase without the overhead of building them manually.

First is Skiper UI. This is my current go-to for design-heavy sections like pricing cards or modern layouts where aesthetics need to be the main focus.

Second is Animaster Lib. If you need motion elements like scroll animations or complex hover states, this library has over 250 components ready to ship.

Third is ThreeUI. This is for the immersive side of things. It uses WebGL and shaders to build landing pages that feel spatial and dynamic.

I am currently using Claude as part of my workflow to help integrate these into my projects. It saves me from writing boilerplate and lets me focus on the actual logic of the app instead of just the visual polish.

All of these allow you to copy components directly into your project files, which is significantly faster than trying to hack together a custom animation library from zero.

If you are stuck choosing between a clean portfolio site and a high-conversion SaaS landing page, these tools make the difference between spending three days or three hours on the front end.

If you want the links to these libraries, just comment Library below and I will send them over.

What is your go-to workflow for adding animations to a site without destroying your performance scores?

